prefix over the canonical CBOR of\n-the body, never an unspecified default. Equal bodies are one row. The id\n+any traversal (`:yin.k/undecodable`), so every walk is over a map from\n+id to one row. The gate's walk over install children follows only\n+`:install` child crefs and keeps an **active recursion-path set**: a\n+cref that leaves the rows, or a child that is already on the active\n+path, stops the gate with `:yin.k/undecodable`. A child reached again\n+*off* the active path is not an error: two install entries whose\n+children have identical content (two identical halted children, say)\n+share one child row by Merkle construction, the structural graph is a\n+DAG, and each install entry still restores as its own native task\n+instance with its own task-local cells and its own contextual custody\n+validation. Completed content validation is cached per row; instance\n+restoration never is. When a pack is addressed (§10.2) this map is what\n+is hashed, and a `:yin.k/root` that does not name a `:task` row in\n+`:rows` is `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n+\n+**Content rows** have `id = (dao.jing/segment-key body)` under the one\n+digest this carrier pins, SHA-256, the same digest the semantic profile's\n+code addresses `:segment/sha256-…` use; an id carrying any other\n+multihash is `:yin.k/undecodable`. The carrier fixes the digest rather\n+than dispatching on each supplied address, because a canonical address\n+scheme must be unique to be canonical. Equal bodies are one row. The id\n is Merkle: it commits to every row id in the body.\n \n **Identity rows** are cells (UCF §7.5.3). A cell has a task-local id `C`\n@@ -138,7 +150,7 @@ root and per install child.\n   census entry. Merkle construction cannot build a cref cycle bottom-up,\n   and a `:cell-body` references only a stream (content) and a position\n   (data), so no cell closes a cycle. The receiver still checks\n-  acyclicity over this family explicitly (§8 step 5), because a receiver\n+  acyclicity over this family explicitly (§8 step 3), because a receiver\n   validates, it does not trust construction.\n - *Context lookup edges*: a `:parked-ref` row names a parked id `P`\n   resolved through the task's parked table; a closure's `store-of` names\n@@ -181,14 +193,16 @@ are not admitted by this carrier and refuse as `:yin.k/non-portable`\n kind `:byte-string`.\n \n **Metadata** is walked like any other part of a value when deciding both\n-portability and whether a value reaches a reference: retained metadata\n-that holds a cursor reference, a closure, or any non-literal makes its\n-carrier non-literal, and since collection rows have no metadata slot,\n-such a value refuses as `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:metadata` naming the\n-path (copying it as data would carry source coordinates and seals\n-unremapped). Reference-free retained metadata on a literal travels as\n-Jing preserves it and is hashed as Jing hashes it. Metadata a reader\n-strips is not retained metadata and plays no part.\n+portability and whether a value reaches a reference. Reference-free\n+retained metadata on a *literal* travels as Jing preserves it and is\n+hashed as Jing hashes it. Collection rows have no metadata slot, so\n+**any collection that must become a row and carries retained metadata\n+refuses**, whether the metadata holds a reference (copying it as data\n+would carry source coordinates and seals unremapped) or is ordinary\n+reference-free data such as `{:doc \"x\"}`: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n+`:metadata` naming the path. This is a proposed limitation of this\n+carrier, not an inherited UCF requirement (§10). Metadata a reader strips\n+is not retained metadata and plays no part.\n \n A guest map is always wrapped, `[:lit m]` or a `:map` row, and is open\n data inside the wrapper; structural rows are closed.\n@@ -204,11 +218,17 @@ collection reach a reference?*, answered by walking it.\n \n **Structural data** is a second, separate domain: values that UCF\n §7.4.3 requires to be in the canonical-bytes domain but that are not\n-guest literals, namely install responses, request and link envelopes,\n-operation ids, link ids and call ids. A structural-data slot holds plain\n-canonical-bytes data and is validated by the rule that governs it (an\n-install response by the linker's delivery checks, an op-id by §7.4.3\n-r5), not by the literal domain. Table rows mark these slots `D`.\n+guest literals and contain no guest values, namely install responses,\n+link envelopes, operation ids, link ids and call ids. A structural-data\n+slot holds plain canonical-bytes data and is validated by the rule that\n+governs it (an install response by the linker's delivery checks, an\n+op-id by §7.4.3 r5), not by the literal domain. Table rows mark these\n+slots `D`. A retained **FFI request envelope is not structural data**:\n+its arguments are guest values, which the existing encoder encodes\n+recursively (`handoff.cljc`) so that closures stay portable and\n+resource references go through the census; it is a closed row\n+(`:ffi-envelope`, §5.3) whose correlation fields are `D` and whose\n+arguments are `V`.\n \n Positions that hold structural data (addresses, pcs, symbols as binding\n names, reasons, phases, ids) are typed by the row table and are not `V`;\n@@ -218,10 +238,13 @@ their kinds are fixed per slot, as for code rows.\n \n Kinds are closed. An unknown kind, a wrong arity, a slot whose content is\n not of the tabled kind, or a `V` outside §4's union is `:yin.k/undecodable`\n-naming the row and slot. \"Sorted\" means ordered by Jing canonical-byte\n-order of the sort key, and the sort key never contains a cell reference\n-(§6). Ordered slots are part of the schema because they determine the\n-Merkle id.\n+naming the row and slot. **One ordering rule** governs every emitted row\n+and every receiver check: \"sorted\" means ordered by the Jing canonical\n+bytes of the sort key *as spelled in the emitted row*, cell numbers\n+included, under the final numbering of §6. The class-erased skeletons of\n+§6 are used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the\n+order of an emitted row. Ordered slots are part of the schema because\n+they determine the Merkle id.\n \n `N` is an exact CBOR integer in `[0, 2^52−1]` (amendment §2). `A` is a\n code address, `M` a manifest address, `P` a parked id, `C` a cell id,\n@@ -229,9 +252,9 @@ code address, `M` a manifest address, `P` a parked id, `C` a cell id,\n only the row kinds its table entry names; a `[:row B]` whose target is\n of another kind refuses (§8). Keyed rows (`:bindings`, `:map`, `:store`,\n `:parked-table`, `:cells`, the installs and module-stores slots) must\n-have unique keys under canonical bytes **and** under portable decoded\n-equality, since Jing's own map and set rules do not apply to a key list\n-spelled as a vector.\n+have unique keys, and `:set` rows unique elements, under canonical bytes\n+**and** under portable decoded equality, since Jing's own map and set\n+rules do not apply to a key or element list spelled as a vector.\n \n ### 5.1 Values (content rows)\n \n@@ -239,8 +262,8 @@ spelled as a vector.\n |---|---|---|\n | `:vec` | `V …` in order | vector |\n | `:list` | `V …` in order | list |\n-| `:set` | `V …` sorted by the element's cell-free skeleton (§6) | set |\n-| `:map` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …`, each `kᵢ` a `V`, sorted by the key's cell-free skeleton | map |\n+| `:set` | `V …` sorted (§5 preamble) | set |\n+| `:map` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …`, each `kᵢ` a `V`, sorted by key | map |\n | `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁ S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | `{S V}` |\n | `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | E: the named environment plus, when `store-of` is an `M`, `engine/store-of-key` (amendment §2) |\n | `:closure` | `segment-A  entry-pc-N  params  bindings-cref  store-of` | semantic closure (amendment §6 semantic arm); `params` is a plain vector of symbols checked against the addressed lambda |\n@@ -290,15 +313,24 @@ the amendment r5 required-keys list fixes them; `:park` and\n | `:next` | `cell-C` |\n | `:put` | `stream-cref  value-V  op-id-D` |\n | `:ffi` | `cell-C  call-id-D  op` (op may be nil) |\n-| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D  request-envelope-D  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  response-cell-C  op-id-D` |\n+| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D  envelope-cref  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  response-cell-C  op-id-D` |\n | `:link-request` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C  envelope-D  op-id-D` |\n | `:link-response` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C` |\n | `:install` | `name-S` |\n \n-Correlation must agree, not merely be present: a cell's `:cell-body`\n-stream is the pending's response stream; an envelope's link id or call\n-id equals the pending's; a `:link-request` envelope names the pending's\n-module. `op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\n+The retained FFI request is a closed content row:\n+\n+| Kind | Slots |\n+|---|---|\n+| `:ffi-envelope` | `call-id-D  op  args-cref` where `args-cref` targets a `:vec` row (or the slot holds `[:lit []]` when there are no arguments) |\n+\n+so that the arguments are guest values encoded recursively, closures\n+stay portable, and resource references enter the census. Lower\n+reconstructs the retained request verbatim from these fields and never\n+recomputes the arguments. Correlation must agree, not merely be present:\n+a cell's `:cell-body` stream is the pending's response stream; an\n+envelope's link id or call id equals the pending's; a `:link-request`\n+envelope names the pending's module. `op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\n present exactly when the write was first attempted through a fenced\n writer, carried verbatim, never minted or renumbered by lift or lower,\n and subject to every rule of §7.4.3 r5 (below the root's\n@@ -314,7 +346,7 @@ is `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n \n | Kind | Slots |\n |---|---|\n-| `:store` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …` sorted by the key's cell-free skeleton; the isolated slice of UCF §7.6.2 |\n+| `:store` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …` sorted by key; the isolated slice of UCF §7.6.2 |\n | `:module-store` | `manifest-M  store-cref` |\n | `:parked-table` | `P₁ regs-cref₁ P₂ regs-cref₂ …` sorted by P |\n | `:install` | `name-S  phase  parent-D  response-D  child-cref` |\n@@ -402,51 +434,66 @@ can). The numbering is therefore defined over the **whole alias\n topology** of the task, and the definition is the reference against\n which any algorithm is checked.\n \n-**Definition.** Let the task's cells be `n` in number. For each bijection\n-`σ` from the cells to `{1 … n}`, build the complete row set of §5 with\n-cell `c` spelled `:yin.k/c-σ(c)` (ordered containers in order; sets,\n-maps, stores, bindings and tables sorted by the canonical bytes of their\n-keys *as spelled under σ*), and take the canonical bytes of the root\n-body. The canonical numbering is the `σ` whose root bytes are least\n-under bytewise order. Two `σ` with equal least bytes are automorphisms\n-of the task (they produce identical row sets by construction) and the\n-choice between them is immaterial by definition, not by assumption.\n-\n-**Algorithm.** The lift computes the same `σ` without enumerating all\n-`n!` bijections:\n-\n-1. **Refinement.** Give every cell the signature of its body (stream,\n-   position). Repeat: encode the task with each cell spelled by its\n-   current signature class, in a fixed traversal (waits in order; within\n-   a wait registers then pending; env bindings by symbol, stack\n-   bottom-up, k bottom-to-top; parked table by `P`; store by key;\n-   module stores by `M`; result; installs by name with each child as\n-   its own task), and give each cell the new signature *(old class, the\n-   multiset of positions at which it is referenced, where a position is\n-   the path from the root with unordered containers' elements named by\n-   their own current encoding)*. Stop when the partition into classes\n-   stops changing. This is colour refinement over the reference graph\n-   and terminates in at most `n` rounds.\n-2. **Individualization.** If every class is a singleton, order classes\n-   by their signature's canonical bytes and assign `1 … n`. Otherwise\n-   pick the smallest class under that order, try each of its members as\n-   the next individualized cell, refine again, and recurse; among the\n-   resulting complete numberings take the one with the least root\n-   bytes. Residual classes after full refinement are orbits of the\n-   task's automorphism group, so every choice within a class yields the\n-   same root bytes and the search is bounded by the symmetry of the\n-   state, which for a parked task is small.\n-\n-Skeletons used inside the refinement keep the full value-union tags:\n-a cell position is spelled `[:cell <class>]`, never a bare class, so it\n-cannot collide with `[:lit [:cell …]]` (§10.9). Context lookups (parked\n-ids, store-of) are not expanded during refinement; they are followed\n-once as edges to their target rows, which are already in the traversal.\n-\n-**Validation.** The receiver recomputes `σ` from the decoded rows and\n-refuses with `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:noncanonical-cells` if the\n-carried numbering differs; contiguity alone is not canonicality. This\n-is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift kind.\n+**What is defined, and what is not claimed.** The canonical numbering\n+is defined as **the output of the procedure below**, which is a function\n+of the state alone: every step depends only on structure, never on a\n+cell's number or on any hash that includes one, so renaming the cells of\n+the input does not change the output. It is *not* claimed to be the\n+minimum of the root bytes over all `n!` bijections; that is a different\n+definition, and a refinement-and-search procedure is not shown to reach\n+it (signature order does not order Merkle bytes). Nor is it claimed that\n+cells left together by refinement are interchangeable: they need not be\n+(a guest set encoding a disjoint triangle and square as ordered pairs of\n+equal-body cells leaves all seven cells in one class, and no\n+automorphism exchanges a triangle vertex with a square vertex). The\n+procedure therefore explores every unresolved alternative, with no\n+pruning, and the representative it returns is canonical because the\n+procedure is deterministic and renaming-invariant, not because of any\n+symmetry argument.\n+\n+**The procedure.** Let the cells be `n` in number.\n+\n+1. **Class encoding.** A *class encoding* of a value is its canonical\n+   bytes with every cell reference spelled `[:cell <class>]`, the full\n+   value-union tag retained (§10.9), where `<class>` is the cell's\n+   current class label; unordered containers inside it are ordered by\n+   the class encodings of their elements; context lookups (parked ids,\n+   store-of) are not expanded, they appear as the plain `P` or `M`. No\n+   Merkle id enters a class encoding: rows are expanded structurally\n+   for this purpose, and the expansion is bounded by the row DAG.\n+2. **Refinement.** Initial class of a cell: the canonical bytes of its\n+   body (stream marker, position). Repeat: for each cell, its new\n+   signature is the pair *(old class, the sorted multiset of the\n+   positions at which it is referenced)*, a position being the path from\n+   the root in a fixed traversal (waits in order; within a wait\n+   registers then pending; env bindings by symbol, stack bottom-up, k\n+   bottom-to-top; parked table by `P`; store by key; module stores by\n+   `M`; result; installs by module name with each child as its own task)\n+   in which an unordered container's element is named by its class\n+   encoding. New class labels are the signatures' canonical bytes,\n+   ordered bytewise. Stop when the partition stops changing. Keeping\n+   the old class in the signature makes refinement monotone, so the\n+   partition stabilizes in at most `n − k` splitting rounds from `k`\n+   initial classes plus one stability check; this bounds partition\n+   rounds only, not signature-processing or search work.\n+3. **Search.** If every class is a singleton, assign `1 … n` in class\n+   order and stop. Otherwise take the first non-singleton class in\n+   class order and, **for each of its members in turn**, give that\n+   member a fresh singleton class ordered before all others, refine to\n+   stability, and recurse. Every branch is explored. Each complete\n+   leaf yields a numbering; build the §5 rows under it and take the\n+   root's Merkle bytes as the leaf's key. The canonical numbering is the\n+   leaf with the least key. Two leaves with equal keys have equal\n+   complete commitments and are immaterial under the digest assumption.\n+\n+**Validation.** The receiver recomputes the procedure from the decoded\n+rows and refuses with `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:noncanonical-cells` if\n+the carried numbering differs; contiguity alone is not canonicality.\n+This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift kind. A receiver\n+whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the procedure completes\n+refuses with a structured resource refusal before any attachment and\n+never labels the state `:noncanonical-cells`, never falls back to\n+encounter order, and never accepts an unfinished check.\n \n Under this rule two lifts of one state are byte-identical. Two lifts of\n different states sharing a closure over the same cell may number it\n@@ -494,35 +541,42 @@ one attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n    and all profiles equal to the root's. Malformed task structure is\n    `:yin.k/undecodable`; an unsupported version or profile is\n    `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; the two are never conflated.\n-2. **Structure.** Every row's kind is tabled with the right arity; every\n-   structural slot holds its tabled kind; every `[:row B]` targets a row\n-   of a kind admissible in that position; every `V` is in §4's union,\n-   every literal in the portable domain, every `D` in the canonical\n-   bytes domain; exactly one row has role `:root`; kind rules of §5.5\n-   hold; the custody slot matches the role, kind and policy table.\n-3. **Canonical form.** Every sorted slot is in its tabled order; every\n-   keyed row has unique keys under canonical bytes and under portable\n-   decoded equality; optional absence has its one spelling (nil in the\n-   tabled slot); a reference-free collection is spelled `[:lit d]`, never\n-   a row; empty collections are literals; metadata obeys §4. A breach is\n-   `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row and slot. Without this step two\n-   different valid encodings could lower to one state, or a `:bindings`\n-   row could carry one symbol twice and lose a binding on lower.\n-4. **Hashes.** Every content row's id equals the address of its body\n-   under the digest the id's multihash names; every code payload hashes\n-   to its key `A` and is well formed under the stamp; the requested body\n-   address, if one was given, equals the root id. Mismatch is\n-   `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the row or code key.\n-5. **Graph.** The structural edge family is acyclic; every row is\n-   reachable from the root through structural edges (an unreachable row\n-   is `:yin.k/undecodable`); every `[:cell C]` names a census entry and\n-   every census entry is reached **through the task's own roots, the\n-   census's declaration edges excluded**, per task and per child; every\n-   `:parked-ref` and `parked-id` resolves in the parked table; every\n-   `store-of` resolves to a carried module store; every `:install`\n-   pending has its entry and every entry's child is a `:child` task.\n-6. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 and compare\n-   (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cells`).\n+2. **Structure, non-recursive.** Every row's kind is tabled with the\n+   right arity; every structural slot holds its tabled kind by local\n+   inspection; every `V` has one of the three tags; every `D` is in the\n+   canonical bytes domain; exactly one row has role `:root`; kind rules\n+   of §5.5 hold; the custody slot matches the role, kind and policy\n+   table. Nothing in this step follows a reference.\n+3. **Graph.** Every cref and every `[:row B]` names a present row of a\n+   kind admissible in that position; the structural edge family is\n+   acyclic (checked with an active-path walk, bounded by the row count);\n+   every row is reachable from the root through structural edges (an\n+   unreachable row is `:yin.k/undecodable`); every `[:cell C]` names a\n+   census entry and every census entry is reached **through the task's\n+   own roots, the census's declaration edges excluded**, per task and\n+   per child; every `:parked-ref` and `parked-id` resolves in the parked\n+   table; every `store-of` resolves to a carried module store; every\n+   `:install` pending has its entry and every entry's child is a\n+   `:child` task. After this step every traversal below is finite and\n+   safe.\n+4. **Hashes.** Every content row's id equals the SHA-256 address of its\n+   body; every code payload hashes to its key `A` and is well formed\n+   under the stamp; the requested body address, if one was given, equals\n+   the root id. Mismatch is `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the row or\n+   code key.\n+5. **Canonical form, recursive.** Every literal is in the portable\n+   domain; every sorted slot is in the §5 order; every keyed row has\n+   unique keys, and every `:set` row unique elements, under canonical\n+   bytes and under portable decoded equality; optional absence has its\n+   one spelling (nil in the tabled slot); a reference-free collection is\n+   spelled `[:lit d]`, never a row; empty collections are literals;\n+   metadata obeys §4. A breach is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row\n+   and slot. Without this step two different valid encodings could lower\n+   to one state, or a `:bindings` row could carry one symbol twice and\n+   lose a binding on lower.\n+6. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 under the work bound and\n+   compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cells`; or the\n+   resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound is exceeded).\n 7. **Code and frames.** Each wait's resume pc is a static safepoint\n    whose kinds admit the wait's reason (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n    `:reason-mismatch`); a parked record's pc is an explicit-park\n@@ -550,10 +604,12 @@ one attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n     the receiving composition.\n 12. **Restore.** Reconstruct isolated code spaces, fresh values, the\n     isolated store, module-store instances (first link wins), one\n-    private resource entry per cell, the parked table, children first,\n-    then waits in order; adopt `max(local, carried)` for the id counter;\n-    publish the machine only after every child succeeds; run, poll,\n-    mint, append, close or initialize nothing during lower.\n+    private resource entry per cell, the parked table, children first\n+    (one native task instance **per install entry**, even where two\n+    entries share a child row), then waits in order; adopt `max(local,\n+    carried)` for the id counter; publish the machine only after every\n+    child succeeds; run, poll, mint, append, close or initialize nothing\n+    during lower.\n \n Diagnostics use the established families and no new top-level outcome:\n `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/undecodable` with `:yin.k/path` as\n@@ -665,15 +721,34 @@ Answers 7–9 are the Architect's (round 2) and are adopted:\n 9. **Skeleton placeholder.** Safe only if the full value-union tag is\n    retained in the skeleton, which §6 now requires (`[:cell <class>]`,\n    never a bare class); and a placeholder does not canonicalize alias\n-   topology, which is why §6 is now defined by least encoding over\n-   renamings with refinement as the algorithm.\n-\n-Still open after this revision:\n-\n-10. The complexity bound of §6's individualization step on adversarial\n-    inputs (a lift is the emitter's own cost, but a receiver recomputes\n-    it; a receiver may need a work bound and a refusal for states whose\n-    symmetry exceeds it).\n+   topology, which is why §6 is now defined as the output of a\n+   renaming-invariant refinement-and-search procedure.\n+\n+10. **Receiver work bound** (Architect, round 3; adopted as a\n+    requirement, with the limits left to implementation and\n+    composition). Before an implementation brief, the receiver must have\n+    a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cells, traversal depth, refinement\n+    work and search nodes for §6, with elapsed time as an optional\n+    operational limit on top. Exceeding a local budget produces a\n+    structured resource or capability refusal before any attachment, in\n+    the qualified-unsupported family `datom.world.md` already provides;\n+    it never labels a valid state `:noncanonical-cells`, never falls\n+    back to encounter order, and never accepts an unfinished check. The\n+    exact limits and their refusal mapping are implementation and\n+    composition decisions and must not alter canonical bytes.\n+\n+**Proposals, not consequences.** The following are design choices this\n+document makes; they follow neither uniquely from the owner's three\n+rulings nor from the six invariants, and an implementation brief must\n+name them as choices being accepted: a new body version (3) rather than\n+another carrier identification; the canonical representative of §6 as\n+defined by its procedure; the wire unit and pack framing of §3; the\n+explicit fork-policy spelling of §5.5; semantic-only initial scope; the\n+byte-string and metadata restrictions of §4; and positional rows over\n+EAV (§1).\n+\n+Still open after this revision: none beyond the other-profile tables\n+(§10.7) and the work-bound limits (§10.10).\n \n ## 11. What this does not change\n \n7e24fa3a7de016df2b4dcd00a9f74e30b1cf6cf8\n     1\t# UCF transported state as tuples: the row carrier (body version 3)\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: **Draft, proposed, not landed.** Written 2026-10-10 from the owner\n     4\tdiscussion recorded in\n     5\t`public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog`\n     6\tPart Three; revised the same day after the codex Architect review\n     7\t`collab/1791500000000-architect-ucf-transport-tuples-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n     8\t(round 1: thirteen findings; round 2: eight; round 3: seven; all\n     9\taddressed below, and nine answers adopted in §10). §10 also lists which\n    10\tparts of this document are design proposals rather than consequences of\n    11\tthe owner's rulings.\n    12\tSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), to UCF §7 in\n    13\t[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md),\n    14\tand to the version-2 amendment\n    15\t[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md),\n    16\twhose body is the fact list this carrier must reproduce. Follows the row\n    17\tconventions of [`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md).\n    18\tNothing here is implemented. Body versions 0, 1 and 2 keep their grammar,\n    19\treaders and bytes; this document proposes a fourth body version whose\n    20\tcarrier is rows, and says what it must preserve.\n    21\t\n    22\t## 1. Stance\n    23\t\n    24\tThree owner rulings fix the shape of this document.\n    25\t\n    26\t1. *Cross-VM safepoints are semantic-VM safepoints.* The Universal AST is\n    27\t   the canonical code; the instruction vector is its deterministic\n    28\t   lowering; a safepoint is `(segment-address, pc)` into that vector\n    29\t   (UCF §7.3.2, §7.4.1). This carrier changes nothing about code\n    30\t   identity, safepoints, or execution profiles.\n    31\t2. *The transported state is a tuple set.* Sharing and the literal/marker\n    32\t   distinction stop being special cases: a reference is a row id in a\n    33\t   typed position, sharing is Merkle, and a program's data is wrapped\n    34\t   where it can be confused with a reference. What does not collapse is\n    35\t   the per-entity decision between content and identity, which this\n    36\t   carrier makes a property of the row kind and then commits to the root\n    37\t   through a census.\n    38\t3. *Tuples are the transport form only.* They exist between a lift and a\n    39\t   lower. At rest they are rows like any other and queryable by the\n    40\t   ordinary interpreter. Nothing executes them; the destination lowers\n    41\t   them into its native representation.\n    42\t\n    43\tThis proposal chooses positional rows: a row kind fixes a slot list, one\n    44\ttable serves encoder and decoder, and a row's Merkle id commits to its\n    45\tchildren inline, as code rows already do. EAV is expressive enough and\n    46\tnothing in the invariants rules it out; positional rows are chosen for\n    47\tcompactness, closedness, and consistency with the existing row\n    48\tconventions, not because EAV was rejected by review.\n    49\t\n    50\t## 2. Positioning: body version 3, same profiles\n    51\t\n    52\tBody versions 0 and 1 (UCF §7.2.1) and 2 (the amendment) are nested\n    53\tcanonical-CBOR maps. **This carrier is body version 3.** The outer\n    54\t`:yin.k/version` gate of §7.2.1 and amendment §8 step 1 runs first and\n    55\tunchanged: a reader that does not speak 3 refuses with\n    56\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch` carrying the version found and the set it\n    57\tspeaks, before any row is read. A version-3 reader keeps versions 0, 1\n    58\tand 2 exactly as their contracts state and never upgrades a body across\n    59\tversions.\n    60\t\n    61\tThe execution profile is untouched. `:yin.k/contract` carries exactly the\n    62\tamendment §1 profile map `{:yin.k/engine e :yin.code/contract c\n    63\t:yin.k/version 1}`, from the same registry, under the same whole-tree\n    64\tgate (one profile per task and install tree). Code stamps `\"v3\"`, `\"b2\"`,\n    65\t`\"r2\"` and the inner version 1 do not move: this is a change of carrier,\n    66\tnot of instruction, scheduler, or value semantics, exactly the\n    67\tdistinction the amendment draws between its versions and executable\n    68\tcode.\n    69\t\n    70\tThis document tables the **semantic** profile's registers, frames and\n    71\tclosures in full, and **version 3 declares support for the semantic\n    72\tprofile only**: a version-3 body whose profile map names `:stack`,\n    73\t`:register` or `:walker` fails closed at the profile gate\n    74\t(`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`) until those profiles' row tables and\n    75\tvalidation rules are published against amendment §5.2–5.4, with their\n    76\tcode addresses and profile maps unchanged. Like version 2,\n    77\tversion 3 transports native suspended state within a profile and does not\n    78\ttranslate between kernels. The foreign-engine case (an engine that\n    79\timplements canonical semantic-VM execution in its own layout, UCF §7.4.2)\n    80\tis the semantic profile lowered through that engine's value profile, and\n    81\tvalue profiles are a separate document (§10.6).\n    82\t\n    83\t## 3. Rows, id spaces, and the census\n    84\t\n    85\tEvery row is `[id kind & slots]`; the stored payload is the body\n    86\t`[kind & slots]`; the id is an address envelope outside the hashed bytes,\n    87\tas for code rows (code-as-tuples §2.1).\n    88\t\n    89\t**The wire unit** is one closed map, so that the gate of §8 has\n    90\tsomething to run on before any row is interpreted:\n    91\t\n    92\t```clojure\n    93\t{:yin.k/handoff true\n    94\t :yin.k/version 3\n    95\t :yin.k/root    B          ; the root :task row's id\n    96\t :yin.k/rows    [[id kind & slots] …]}   ; every row of the tree, any order\n    97\t```\n    98\t\n    99\tUnlisted keys refuse. Duplicate ids among `:yin.k/rows` refuse before\n   100\tany traversal (`:yin.k/undecodable`), so every walk is over a map from\n   101\tid to one row. The gate's walk over install children follows only\n   102\t`:install` child crefs and keeps an **active recursion-path set**: a\n   103\tcref that leaves the rows, or a child that is already on the active\n   104\tpath, stops the gate with `:yin.k/undecodable`. A child reached again\n   105\t*off* the active path is not an error: two install entries whose\n   106\tchildren have identical content (two identical halted children, say)\n   107\tshare one child row by Merkle construction, the structural graph is a\n   108\tDAG, and each install entry still restores as its own native task\n   109\tinstance with its own task-local cells and its own contextual custody\n   110\tvalidation. Completed content validation is cached per row; instance\n   111\trestoration never is. When a pack is addressed (§10.2) this map is what\n   112\tis hashed, and a `:yin.k/root` that does not name a `:task` row in\n   113\t`:rows` is `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n   114\t\n   115\t**Content rows** have `id = (dao.jing/segment-key body)` under the one\n   116\tdigest this carrier pins, SHA-256, the same digest the semantic profile's\n   117\tcode addresses `:segment/sha256-…` use; an id carrying any other\n   118\tmultihash is `:yin.k/undecodable`. The carrier fixes the digest rather\n   119\tthan dispatching on each supplied address, because a canonical address\n   120\tscheme must be unique to be canonical. Equal bodies are one row. The id\n   121\tis Merkle: it commits to every row id in the body.\n   122\t\n   123\t**Identity rows** are cells (UCF §7.5.3). A cell has a task-local id `C`\n   124\tin the form `:yin.k/c-<n>`, minted by the lift under §6's canonical\n   125\tnumbering, never content-addressed, and scoped to the task whose root\n   126\tcarries it: a cell id means nothing outside its root, in a pool or a\n   127\tquery, and two tasks' `:yin.k/c-1` are unrelated.\n   128\t\n   129\t**The census.** A cell's *contents* (its stream marker and kept position)\n   130\tare not reachable through its id, so a content row that references a\n   131\tcell commits to the cell's identity but not to what the cell holds. The\n   132\troot therefore carries a **cell census**, a content row\n   133\t\n   134\t```clojure\n   135\t[X :cells  C₁ B₁  C₂ B₂  …]          ; sorted by n; Bᵢ is a cref\n   136\t[Bᵢ :cell-body  stream-cref  position]\n   137\t```\n   138\t\n   139\tthat maps every cell id to the content row of its body. The census is a\n   140\tslot of the root, so the root id commits to every cell's contents. A\n   141\tchange to any cell's position or stream changes `Bᵢ`, the census, and\n   142\tthe root. Identity references preserve aliasing; the census\n   143\tauthenticates contents. The validation rules (§8) require the census to\n   144\tlist exactly the cells the task reaches: none missing, none extra, per\n   145\troot and per install child.\n   146\t\n   147\t**Acyclicity.** Two edge families exist and are checked separately:\n   148\t\n   149\t- *Structural value edges*: a cref in a slot, a cell iref in a value, a\n   150\t  census entry. Merkle construction cannot build a cref cycle bottom-up,\n   151\t  and a `:cell-body` references only a stream (content) and a position\n   152\t  (data), so no cell closes a cycle. The receiver still checks\n   153\t  acyclicity over this family explicitly (§8 step 3), because a receiver\n   154\t  validates, it does not trust construction.\n   155\t- *Context lookup edges*: a `:parked-ref` row names a parked id `P`\n   156\t  resolved through the task's parked table; a closure's `store-of` names\n   157\t  a manifest address `M` resolved through the module stores; an\n   158\t  `:install` pending names a module whose entry holds a child body.\n   159\t  These may close cycles and that is legal: a module store that holds a\n   160\t  closure whose `store-of` is that same module is the ordinary shape of\n   161\t  a linked module, and self- or mutually-referencing parked records\n   162\t  resolve through the explicit `P` table exactly as amendment §6 states.\n   163\t  A guest value that would need a cycle through *structural* edges is\n   164\t  refused at lift as `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:cyclic` naming the\n   165\t  path, unchanged from UCF §7.5.3.\n   166\t\n   167\t## 4. The value union\n   168\t\n   169\tEvery position that holds a guest value holds one **encoded value** `V`,\n   170\ta two-element vector whose first element is a fixed tag:\n   171\t\n   172\t| `V` | Meaning |\n   173\t|---|---|\n   174\t| `[:lit d]` | a literal: `d` is in the portable literal domain below, carried by Jing as data |\n   175\t| `[:row B]` | a reference to the content row `B` (a closure, a collection row, a frame value, a primitive, a stream marker) |\n   176\t| `[:cell C]` | a reference to the identity cell `C` |\n   177\t\n   178\tThe union is disjoint by the tag, not by the shape or spelling of `d`: a\n   179\tliteral keyword that happens to equal a row address is `[:lit k]` and\n   180\tcan never be read as a reference; a literal vector that looks like a\n   181\t`[:row …]` pair is `[:lit [...]]`. No decision depends on content, which\n   182\tis the UCF §7.5.1 property restated for rows.\n   183\t\n   184\t**The portable literal domain** is UCF §7.5.1's scalar and collection\n   185\tarms, closed under nesting, with exactly the numeric classes Jing and\n   186\t`value-encoder` already admit: nil, booleans, exact integers of any width\n   187\t(Jing major types 0/1, tags 2/3), float64 carriers, Jing's Decimal and\n   188\tRational carriers under Jing's normalization rules, strings, keywords,\n   189\tsymbols, and vectors, lists, sets and maps *all of whose elements, keys,\n   190\tvalues and retained metadata are in the domain*. Carrier classes are\n   191\tretained, never coerced (an integral float stays a float). Byte strings\n   192\tare not admitted by this carrier and refuse as `:yin.k/non-portable`\n   193\tkind `:byte-string`.\n   194\t\n   195\t**Metadata** is walked like any other part of a value when deciding both\n   196\tportability and whether a value reaches a reference. Reference-free\n   197\tretained metadata on a *literal* travels as Jing preserves it and is\n   198\thashed as Jing hashes it. Collection rows have no metadata slot, so\n   199\t**any collection that must become a row and carries retained metadata\n   200\trefuses**, whether the metadata holds a reference (copying it as data\n   201\twould carry source coordinates and seals unremapped) or is ordinary\n   202\treference-free data such as `{:doc \"x\"}`: `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n   203\t`:metadata` naming the path. This is a proposed limitation of this\n   204\tcarrier, not an inherited UCF requirement (§10). Metadata a reader strips\n   205\tis not retained metadata and plays no part.\n   206\t\n   207\tA guest map is always wrapped, `[:lit m]` or a `:map` row, and is open\n   208\tdata inside the wrapper; structural rows are closed.\n   209\t\n   210\tA collection whose elements, keys, values or metadata include a\n   211\treference is not a literal; it becomes a row (§5.1) and the position\n   212\tholds `[:row B]`. A collection that reaches no reference is a literal\n   213\tand **must** be `[:lit d]`: a reference-free `:vec`, `:list`, `:set` or\n   214\t`:map` row is a non-canonical spelling and refuses (§8). Empty\n   215\tcollections are therefore always literals. This is the only structural\n   216\tdecision the encoder makes and it is decided by one question, *does this\n   217\tcollection reach a reference?*, answered by walking it.\n   218\t\n   219\t**Structural data** is a second, separate domain: values that UCF\n   220\t§7.4.3 requires to be in the canonical-bytes domain but that are not\n   221\tguest literals and contain no guest values, namely install responses,\n   222\tlink envelopes, operation ids, link ids and call ids. A structural-data\n   223\tslot holds plain canonical-bytes data and is validated by the rule that\n   224\tgoverns it (an install response by the linker's delivery checks, an\n   225\top-id by §7.4.3 r5), not by the literal domain. Table rows mark these\n   226\tslots `D`. A retained **FFI request envelope is not structural data**:\n   227\tits arguments are guest values, which the existing encoder encodes\n   228\trecursively (`handoff.cljc`) so that closures stay portable and\n   229\tresource references go through the census; it is a closed row\n   230\t(`:ffi-envelope`, §5.3) whose correlation fields are `D` and whose\n   231\targuments are `V`.\n   232\t\n   233\tPositions that hold structural data (addresses, pcs, symbols as binding\n   234\tnames, reasons, phases, ids) are typed by the row table and are not `V`;\n   235\ttheir kinds are fixed per slot, as for code rows.\n   236\t\n   237\t## 5. The row table\n   238\t\n   239\tKinds are closed. An unknown kind, a wrong arity, a slot whose content is\n   240\tnot of the tabled kind, or a `V` outside §4's union is `:yin.k/undecodable`\n   241\tnaming the row and slot. **One ordering rule** governs every emitted row\n   242\tand every receiver check: \"sorted\" means ordered by the Jing canonical\n   243\tbytes of the sort key *as spelled in the emitted row*, cell numbers\n   244\tincluded, under the final numbering of §6. The class-erased skeletons of\n   245\t§6 are used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the\n   246\torder of an emitted row. Ordered slots are part of the schema because\n   247\tthey determine the Merkle id.\n   248\t\n   249\t`N` is an exact CBOR integer in `[0, 2^52−1]` (amendment §2). `A` is a\n   250\tcode address, `M` a manifest address, `P` a parked id, `C` a cell id,\n   251\t`S` a symbol, `D` structural data (§4). Each `[:row B]` position admits\n   252\tonly the row kinds its table entry names; a `[:row B]` whose target is\n   253\tof another kind refuses (§8). Keyed rows (`:bindings`, `:map`, `:store`,\n   254\t`:parked-table`, `:cells`, the installs and module-stores slots) must\n   255\thave unique keys, and `:set` rows unique elements, under canonical bytes\n   256\t**and** under portable decoded equality, since Jing's own map and set\n   257\trules do not apply to a key or element list spelled as a vector.\n   258\t\n   259\t### 5.1 Values (content rows)\n   260\t\n   261\t| Kind | Slots | Decodes to |\n   262\t|---|---|---|\n   263\t| `:vec` | `V …` in order | vector |\n   264\t| `:list` | `V …` in order | list |\n   265\t| `:set` | `V …` sorted (§5 preamble) | set |\n   266\t| `:map` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …`, each `kᵢ` a `V`, sorted by key | map |\n   267\t| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁ S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | `{S V}` |\n   268\t| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | E: the named environment plus, when `store-of` is an `M`, `engine/store-of-key` (amendment §2) |\n   269\t| `:closure` | `segment-A  entry-pc-N  params  bindings-cref  store-of` | semantic closure (amendment §6 semantic arm); `params` is a plain vector of symbols checked against the addressed lambda |\n   270\t| `:prim` | `name-S` | the named primitive after the profile check (UCF §7.5.2); the profile lives in requires |\n   271\t| `:stream` | `identity  descriptor` | a stream marker; both plain, authenticated at lift and re-sealed at lower |\n   272\t| `:reified` | `regs-cref` | `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/frame :yin.k/registers R}`: a captured continuation, no wait |\n   273\t| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/frame :yin.k/parked-id P}`: resolves in the task's parked table; never duplicates `R` |\n   274\t\n   275\t`:reified` and `:parked-ref` keep the two outer discriminants amendment\n   276\t§6 requires and lower differently: a reified value becomes a\n   277\treceiver-owned native continuation from `R`; a parked reference\n   278\treconstructs the engine's parked value for `P`. A reified value's\n   279\tregisters are validated in captured-value context (amendment §7): its pc\n   280\tmust be a `:current-continuation` site's successor, not a wait or park\n   281\tsafepoint.\n   282\t\n   283\t### 5.2 Semantic registers, waits, frames (content rows)\n   284\t\n   285\t| Kind | Slots |\n   286\t|---|---|\n   287\t| `:regs` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  stack-cref  k-cref` |\n   288\t| `:stack` | `V …` bottom first |\n   289\t| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` bottom to top |\n   290\t| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  stack-base-N` |\n   291\t| `:wait` | `regs-cref  pending-cref` |\n   292\t\n   293\t`:regs` is amendment §5.1's `R` for the semantic profile, and nothing\n   294\telse: no reason, no pending. A wait is a `:wait` row pairing registers\n   295\twith a pending; a reified value and a parked record reference `:regs`\n   296\tdirectly. `:kframe` is exactly the semantic return frame `{:type :return\n   297\tsegment pc env stack-base}`; the semantic profile has no other frame\n   298\ttype on the wire (walker effect frames belong to the walker profile's\n   299\town table). Resume pc and return pc must lie inside their segment, the\n   300\treturn pc immediately after a non-tail call, stack bases nondecreasing\n   301\tfrom outermost to innermost and each at most the enclosing stack length\n   302\t(amendment §5.1), all checked in §8.\n   303\t\n   304\t### 5.3 Pending (content rows)\n   305\t\n   306\tOne kind, `:pending`, whose first slot is the reason and whose remaining\n   307\tslots are fixed per reason. The reasons are UCF §7.4.3's closed union as\n   308\tthe amendment r5 required-keys list fixes them; `:park` and\n   309\t`:call-effect` are not reasons and refuse as `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n   310\t\n   311\t| reason | slots after the reason |\n   312\t|---|---|\n   313\t| `:next` | `cell-C` |\n   314\t| `:put` | `stream-cref  value-V  op-id-D` |\n   315\t| `:ffi` | `cell-C  call-id-D  op` (op may be nil) |\n   316\t| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D  envelope-cref  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  response-cell-C  op-id-D` |\n   317\t| `:link-request` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C  envelope-D  op-id-D` |\n   318\t| `:link-response` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C` |\n   319\t| `:install` | `name-S` |\n   320\t\n   321\tThe retained FFI request is a closed content row:\n   322\t\n   323\t| Kind | Slots |\n   324\t|---|---|\n   325\t| `:ffi-envelope` | `call-id-D  op  args-cref` where `args-cref` targets a `:vec` row (or the slot holds `[:lit []]` when there are no arguments) |\n   326\t\n   327\tso that the arguments are guest values encoded recursively, closures\n   328\tstay portable, and resource references enter the census. Lower\n   329\treconstructs the retained request verbatim from these fields and never\n   330\trecomputes the arguments. Correlation must agree, not merely be present:\n   331\ta cell's `:cell-body` stream is the pending's response stream; an\n   332\tenvelope's link id or call id equals the pending's; a `:link-request`\n   333\tenvelope names the pending's module. `op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\n   334\tpresent exactly when the write was first attempted through a fenced\n   335\twriter, carried verbatim, never minted or renumbered by lift or lower,\n   336\tand subject to every rule of §7.4.3 r5 (below the root's\n   337\t`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, unique across the task and its children, absent\n   338\twhen the body has no origin). `request-envelope` and `envelope` are the\n   339\tretained requests, retried verbatim. `call-id` and `link-id` are plain.\n   340\tAn `:install` pending is never sufficient alone: the root's installs\n   341\tslot must hold the entry for `name`, else lift refuses\n   342\t`:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:incomplete-install` and a body carrying it\n   343\tis `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n   344\t\n   345\t### 5.4 Task context (content rows)\n   346\t\n   347\t| Kind | Slots |\n   348\t|---|---|\n   349\t| `:store` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …` sorted by key; the isolated slice of UCF §7.6.2 |\n   350\t| `:module-store` | `manifest-M  store-cref` |\n   351\t| `:parked-table` | `P₁ regs-cref₁ P₂ regs-cref₂ …` sorted by P |\n   352\t| `:install` | `name-S  phase  parent-D  response-D  child-cref` |\n   353\t| `:cells` | the census, §3 |\n   354\t| `:cell-body` | `stream-cref  position` (position in the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` portable cursor domain) |\n   355\t\n   356\tResource entries never appear in `:store`; streams and cells lower into\n   357\tthe receiver's private resource table beside the program store (linker\n   358\tM4, `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3). `:module-store` rows are the per-module\n   359\tsnapshots of §7.6.2's M4 paragraph. The parked table holds exactly the\n   360\treachable records. An `:install` row carries the complete entry of\n   361\t§7.4.3 r5: phase `:running` or `:parked`, parent link id (nil where the\n   362\tcontract permits), the response verbatim as structural data, and the\n   363\tchild as a cref to a whole `:task` row in the child role. \"Verified\"\n   364\tmeans verified by the **receiver**, never trusted because it decodes:\n   365\tthe response's manifest names the module the row is keyed by, its link\n   366\tid equals `parent`, its image is well formed, hashes to the address it\n   367\tis delivered under, is the image the manifest names, and its format,\n   368\tcontract and the child's profile agree (`yin.vm.linker.md` §5, §9;\n   369\tamendment §2). A failure is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row.\n   370\t\n   371\t### 5.5 The root\n   372\t\n   373\t```clojure\n   374\t[T :task\n   375\t   version          ; 3                       (gated first, §8)\n   376\t   contract         ; the profile map, plain  (amendment §1)\n   377\t   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n   378\t   role             ; :root | :child\n   379\t   id-counter       ; N                       (UCF §7.6.3)\n   380\t   store-cref\n   381\t   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   382\t   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   383\t   cells-cref       ; :cells census\n   384\t   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map, unchanged\n   385\t   code             ; plain: {A code-payload …}, each payload verified to hash to its key (§7.2.1)\n   386\t   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   387\t   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, IN WAIT ORDER   (:blocked, :parked)\n   388\t   parked-id        ; P or nil                              (:parked only)\n   389\t   result           ; V or nil                              (:halted only)\n   390\t   custody]         ; plain, see below\n   391\t```\n   392\t\n   393\tKind rules are §7.2.1's: `:blocked` has nonempty frames and neither\n   394\tparked-id nor result; `:parked` has frames (possibly empty), a parked-id\n   395\tnaming a key of the parked table, no result; `:halted` has a result and\n   396\tneither frames nor parked-id. Frames restore in carried order and are\n   397\tnever collapsed to one; a task with an empty ready queue may hold several\n   398\tordered waits.\n   399\t\n   400\t`custody` is the custody arm carried as plain data, under the rules of\n   401\t§7.2.1 and amendment §3, by role, kind and policy:\n   402\t\n   403\t| role | kind | policy | `custody` |\n   404\t|---|---|---|---|\n   405\t| root | blocked / parked | exclusive | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on every successor, absent on a first export |\n   406\t| root | blocked / parked | fork | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` |\n   407\t| root | halted | exclusive | `{:yin.k/origin …}` only: a result is not a lease subject |\n   408\t| root | halted | fork | nil: a fork halt has no custody data and a first fork halt has no origin to carry |\n   409\t| child | any | — | nil; validated with its root's occurrence, origin and counter passed down |\n   410\t\n   411\tThe explicit fork policy spelling projects onto the amendment's\n   412\tempty-header fork representation: a version-3 fork root is admitted\n   413\texactly as a version-0 body is, proposes nothing, and is never upgraded\n   414\tto custody; an exclusive-only receiver refuses it as\n   415\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. Admission semantics are the governing ones\n   416\tunchanged; only the spelling is new. No body carries an epoch.\n   417\t\n   418\tThe root is a content row and **its id is the transported state's\n   419\taddress**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\n   420\tare inside it, as the governing rule keeps them (§10.1). The code map is\n   421\tplain data in the root rather than a row kind: each instruction vector\n   422\tkeeps its own address `A` computed exactly as UCF §7.3.2 computes it, and\n   423\tthe carrier verifies, never recomputes, that address.\n   424\t\n   425\t## 6. Canonical cell numbering\n   426\t\n   427\tCell ids must be a function of the state, or two lifts of one state give\n   428\ttwo root ids. Local rules are not enough: a cell's number must depend on\n   429\teverything that distinguishes it, including references to it from\n   430\telsewhere in the task (the review's counterexample: `'a` bound to a set\n   431\tholding cells X and Y with equal bodies, `'b` bound to X; any rule that\n   432\tlooks only at the set and the bodies cannot tell X from Y, but `'b`\n   433\tcan). The numbering is therefore defined over the **whole alias\n   434\ttopology** of the task, and the definition is the reference against\n   435\twhich any algorithm is checked.\n   436\t\n   437\t**What is defined, and what is not claimed.** The canonical numbering\n   438\tis defined as **the output of the procedure below**, which is a function\n   439\tof the state alone: every step depends only on structure, never on a\n   440\tcell's number or on any hash that includes one, so renaming the cells of\n   441\tthe input does not change the output. It is *not* claimed to be the\n   442\tminimum of the root bytes over all `n!` bijections; that is a different\n   443\tdefinition, and a refinement-and-search procedure is not shown to reach\n   444\tit (signature order does not order Merkle bytes). Nor is it claimed that\n   445\tcells left together by refinement are interchangeable: they need not be\n   446\t(a guest set encoding a disjoint triangle and square as ordered pairs of\n   447\tequal-body cells leaves all seven cells in one class, and no\n   448\tautomorphism exchanges a triangle vertex with a square vertex). The\n   449\tprocedure therefore explores every unresolved alternative, with no\n   450\tpruning, and the representative it returns is canonical because the\n   451\tprocedure is deterministic and renaming-invariant, not because of any\n   452\tsymmetry argument.\n   453\t\n   454\t**The procedure.** Let the cells be `n` in number.\n   455\t\n   456\t1. **Class encoding.** A *class encoding* of a value is its canonical\n   457\t   bytes with every cell reference spelled `[:cell <class>]`, the full\n   458\t   value-union tag retained (§10.9), where `<class>` is the cell's\n   459\t   current class label; unordered containers inside it are ordered by\n   460\t   the class encodings of their elements; context lookups (parked ids,\n   461\t   store-of) are not expanded, they appear as the plain `P` or `M`. No\n   462\t   Merkle id enters a class encoding: rows are expanded structurally\n   463\t   for this purpose, and the expansion is bounded by the row DAG.\n   464\t2. **Refinement.** Initial class of a cell: the canonical bytes of its\n   465\t   body (stream marker, position). Repeat: for each cell, its new\n   466\t   signature is the pair *(old class, the sorted multiset of the\n   467\t   positions at which it is referenced)*, a position being the path from\n   468\t   the root in a fixed traversal (waits in order; within a wait\n   469\t   registers then pending; env bindings by symbol, stack bottom-up, k\n   470\t   bottom-to-top; parked table by `P`; store by key; module stores by\n   471\t   `M`; result; installs by module name with each child as its own task)\n   472\t   in which an unordered container's element is named by its class\n   473\t   encoding. New class labels are the signatures' canonical bytes,\n   474\t   ordered bytewise. Stop when the partition stops changing. Keeping\n   475\t   the old class in the signature makes refinement monotone, so the\n   476\t   partition stabilizes in at most `n − k` splitting rounds from `k`\n   477\t   initial classes plus one stability check; this bounds partition\n   478\t   rounds only, not signature-processing or search work.\n   479\t3. **Search.** If every class is a singleton, assign `1 … n` in class\n   480\t   order and stop. Otherwise take the first non-singleton class in\n   481\t   class order and, **for each of its members in turn**, give that\n   482\t   member a fresh singleton class ordered before all others, refine to\n   483\t   stability, and recurse. Every branch is explored. Each complete\n   484\t   leaf yields a numbering; build the §5 rows under it and take the\n   485\t   root's Merkle bytes as the leaf's key. The canonical numbering is the\n   486\t   leaf with the least key. Two leaves with equal keys have equal\n   487\t   complete commitments and are immaterial under the digest assumption.\n   488\t\n   489\t**Validation.** The receiver recomputes the procedure from the decoded\n   490\trows and refuses with `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:noncanonical-cells` if\n   491\tthe carried numbering differs; contiguity alone is not canonicality.\n   492\tThis is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift kind. A receiver\n   493\twhose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the procedure completes\n   494\trefuses with a structured resource refusal before any attachment and\n   495\tnever labels the state `:noncanonical-cells`, never falls back to\n   496\tencounter order, and never accepts an unfinished check.\n   497\t\n   498\tUnder this rule two lifts of one state are byte-identical. Two lifts of\n   499\tdifferent states sharing a closure over the same cell may number it\n   500\tdifferently and so not share that closure's row across lifts; that\n   501\tcross-lift dedup is knowingly given up for identity-bearing values and\n   502\tnever affected rows below a cell. On lower, each `C` becomes one fresh\n   503\tprivate resource entry seeded at its carried position; every `[:cell C]`\n   504\tmaps to that entry; two references to one cell share it and one\n   505\treference per cell keeps its own (UCF §7.5.3).\n   506\t\n   507\t## 7. Equality and the content address\n   508\t\n   509\tThe transported state is a DAG rooted at the `:task` row. Its address is\n   510\tthe root id, which commits to every reachable content row, to every cell\n   511\tid through the references, and to every cell's contents through the\n   512\tcensus. Two transported states are equal iff their root ids are equal,\n   513\tand under §6 that holds iff they were lifted from states equal up to the\n   514\trenaming of cell ids that §6 makes canonical. No physical enumeration of\n   515\tthe row set needs an order, because the root commits to everything and\n   516\tthe pack, if one is addressed, has its own canonical encoding (§10.2).\n   517\tJing supplies canonical bytes and the digest of each body; this document\n   518\tsupplies the order within each body and the numbering of cells. Jing is\n   519\tasked to sort nothing it does not already sort.\n   520\t\n   521\tOccurrence and origin are inside the hash, as in versions 1 and 2 where\n   522\tthe body address is the hash of the whole body. Snapshot variants of one\n   523\toccurrence may therefore have distinct addresses while remaining one\n   524\toccurrence, exactly as UCF §7.3.4 intends.\n   525\t\n   526\t## 8. Validation on lower\n   527\t\n   528\tValidation is pure until the last step and checks the whole tree before\n   529\tone attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n   530\t\n   531\t1. **Gate.** Decode the wire map's canonical bytes retaining numeric\n   532\t   classes; the tag is present and the outer version is the exact\n   533\t   integer 3, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (version found, supported\n   534\t   set). Reject duplicate row ids and a `:yin.k/root` that names no\n   535\t   `:task` row (`:yin.k/undecodable`). Walk install children from the\n   536\t   root in canonical module-name order with a visited set, following\n   537\t   only `:install` child crefs; a cref outside the rows, a revisit, or a\n   538\t   non-`:task` child stops the gate as `:yin.k/undecodable`. Gate every\n   539\t   child's version, then every profile map against the registry (only\n   540\t   the semantic profile is supported, §2), then require all versions 3\n   541\t   and all profiles equal to the root's. Malformed task structure is\n   542\t   `:yin.k/undecodable`; an unsupported version or profile is\n   543\t   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; the two are never conflated.\n   544\t2. **Structure, non-recursive.** Every row's kind is tabled with the\n   545\t   right arity; every structural slot holds its tabled kind by local\n   546\t   inspection; every `V` has one of the three tags; every `D` is in the\n   547\t   canonical bytes domain; exactly one row has role `:root`; kind rules\n   548\t   of §5.5 hold; the custody slot matches the role, kind and policy\n   549\t   table. Nothing in this step follows a reference.\n   550\t3. **Graph.** Every cref and every `[:row B]` names a present row of a\n   551\t   kind admissible in that position; the structural edge family is\n   552\t   acyclic (checked with an active-path walk, bounded by the row count);\n   553\t   every row is reachable from the root through structural edges (an\n   554\t   unreachable row is `:yin.k/undecodable`); every `[:cell C]` names a\n   555\t   census entry and every census entry is reached **through the task's\n   556\t   own roots, the census's declaration edges excluded**, per task and\n   557\t   per child; every `:parked-ref` and `parked-id` resolves in the parked\n   558\t   table; every `store-of` resolves to a carried module store; every\n   559\t   `:install` pending has its entry and every entry's child is a\n   560\t   `:child` task. After this step every traversal below is finite and\n   561\t   safe.\n   562\t4. **Hashes.** Every content row's id equals the SHA-256 address of its\n   563\t   body; every code payload hashes to its key `A` and is well formed\n   564\t   under the stamp; the requested body address, if one was given, equals\n   565\t   the root id. Mismatch is `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the row or\n   566\t   code key.\n   567\t5. **Canonical form, recursive.** Every literal is in the portable\n   568\t   domain; every sorted slot is in the §5 order; every keyed row has\n   569\t   unique keys, and every `:set` row unique elements, under canonical\n   570\t   bytes and under portable decoded equality; optional absence has its\n   571\t   one spelling (nil in the tabled slot); a reference-free collection is\n   572\t   spelled `[:lit d]`, never a row; empty collections are literals;\n   573\t   metadata obeys §4. A breach is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row\n   574\t   and slot. Without this step two different valid encodings could lower\n   575\t   to one state, or a `:bindings` row could carry one symbol twice and\n   576\t   lose a binding on lower.\n   577\t6. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 under the work bound and\n   578\t   compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cells`; or the\n   579\t   resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound is exceeded).\n   580\t7. **Code and frames.** Each wait's resume pc is a static safepoint\n   581\t   whose kinds admit the wait's reason (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n   582\t   `:reason-mismatch`); a parked record's pc is an explicit-park\n   583\t   safepoint; a reified value's pc is a `:current-continuation`\n   584\t   successor; return pcs follow a non-tail call; stack bases are bounded\n   585\t   and nondecreasing; closure params agree with the addressed lambda;\n   586\t   integer slots are in range.\n   587\t8. **Pending and operations.** Reason-specific slot rules; correlation\n   588\t   agreement of §5.3 (cell stream, envelope ids, module names); every\n   589\t   op-id below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without\n   590\t   origin; every install response verified as §5.4 requires.\n   591\t9. **Dependency closure.** Compute the census of amendment §6 from the\n   592\t   rows (waits, pendings and envelopes, parked records, store, module\n   593\t   stores, result, children) and require the carried `requires` and\n   594\t   `code` map to agree with it exactly: a declared segment not reached\n   595\t   or a reached segment not declared is `:yin.k/undecodable`. Then\n   596\t   evaluate `requires` per UCF §7.6.5: missing segments, primitives,\n   597\t   modules, streams, cursor profiles are `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; a body\n   598\t   with cells claims `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; module-store coverage is\n   599\t   complete for every `store-of` reached.\n   600\t10. **Custody.** For an exclusive root, the occurrence, baseline and\n   601\t    operation inspector and admission checks of §7.7.8; an authority\n   602\t    never adds missing header keys.\n   603\t11. **Composition.** Protection, resource, primitive and grant inputs of\n   604\t    the receiving composition.\n   605\t12. **Restore.** Reconstruct isolated code spaces, fresh values, the\n   606\t    isolated store, module-store instances (first link wins), one\n   607\t    private resource entry per cell, the parked table, children first\n   608\t    (one native task instance **per install entry**, even where two\n   609\t    entries share a child row), then waits in order; adopt `max(local,\n   610\t    carried)` for the id counter; publish the machine only after every\n   611\t    child succeeds; run, poll, mint, append, close or initialize nothing\n   612\t    during lower.\n   613\t\n   614\tDiagnostics use the established families and no new top-level outcome:\n   615\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/undecodable` with `:yin.k/path` as\n   616\t`[row-id slot-index …]` and a `:yin.k/kind` where one is named above\n   617\t(`:reason-mismatch`, `:noncanonical-cells`), `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`,\n   618\t`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, and on the lift side `:yin.k/non-portable` with\n   619\tthe two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and\n   620\t`:metadata`.\n   621\t\n   622\t## 9. Lift, lower, and the laws\n   623\t\n   624\t**Lift** (reference machine): refuse unless quiescent; number cells (§6);\n   625\twalk waits, parked records, the slice, module stores, the result and\n   626\teach install child, emitting rows and the census; refuse by path as UCF\n   627\t§7.5.4 and §7.2.1 specify. The nested carrier's value table\n   628\t(`:yin.k/values`) has no counterpart, sharing being Merkle; its cell\n   629\ttable becomes the census.\n   630\t\n   631\t**Lower**: §8 in order, then restore.\n   632\t\n   633\tThe laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n   634\t\n   635\t- **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\n   636\t  version-3 state `S` in the semantic profile **for which `lower(S)`\n   637\t  succeeds** (adequate composition, resource and admission inputs; a\n   638\t  refusal is not a counterexample), and with no execution between lower\n   639\t  and lift, `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the **fresh-receiver\n   640\t  reconstruction context**: the receiver's id counter is 0 so\n   641\t  `max(local, carried)` is the carried value; occurrence, origin and the\n   642\t  custody arm are supplied to the lift from the restored task's own\n   643\t  record, not minted; cell bodies and op-ids are carried verbatim; the\n   644\t  lift's §6 numbering undoes resource remapping. Outside that context\n   645\t  (a receiver with a larger counter, or any step taken) the law does not\n   646\t  apply and native equivalence is the claim.\n   647\t- **Native observational equivalence.** `lower(lift(m))` need not equal\n   648\t  `m` as native state (fresh ids, seals, private keys); under equivalent\n   649\t  external inputs it must preserve the next observable step, the wait\n   650\t  set and its order, the result, and the effect trace.\n   651\t- **Cross-engine.** Running the corpus to each safepoint on an engine\n   652\t  that implements canonical semantic-VM execution yields, under\n   653\t  controlled composition identities (occurrence, origin, resource\n   654\t  descriptors fixed by the harness), the same version-3 row set the\n   655\t  reference machine yields.\n   656\t- **Legacy correspondence.** Versions 0, 1 and 2 are lowered directly by\n   657\t  their own version-aware readers, keeping their addresses and custody\n   658\t  bindings (§10.8). Normalization into version 3 is a separate tool and\n   659\t  is **partial**: a legacy body normalizes with semantic preservation\n   660\t  (same lowered machine under native equivalence) or refuses with one\n   661\t  of this carrier's named refusals (`:byte-string`, `:metadata`, a\n   662\t  profile version 3 does not yet support); it never substitutes a\n   663\t  version-3 address for an admitted legacy checkpoint. Exact mutual\n   664\t  inversion is required only between version 3 and its one canonical\n   665\t  nested projection (§10.3).\n   666\t\n   667\tThe UCF §7.11 harness gains one assertion per law. During transition the\n   668\tStage D/E corpus, re-encoded through the canonical projection, is the\n   669\toracle for the first law.\n   670\t\n   671\t## 10. Questions the review answered, and what remains\n   672\t\n   673\tAnswers 1–6 are the codex Architect's (round 1); where the Architect\n   674\tmarked a point as an owner decision it is labelled so.\n   675\t\n   676\t1. **Occurrence inside the hash.** Kept inside, following the governing\n   677\t   rule and binding custody context to the published checkpoint. An\n   678\t   occurrence-independent execution-state address is an owner-level\n   679\t   protocol decision; if ever pursued it must sit beside, not replace, a\n   680\t   fully authenticated checkpoint address, and it does not substitute\n   681\t   for the census.\n   682\t2. **Packing.** Individual immutable content rows plus a self-contained\n   683\t   transport pack. When a pack is addressed it gets its own address and\n   684\t   canonical encoding, distinct from the semantic root id; cell ids\n   685\t   remain root-scoped inside it. Same answer as code-as-tuples §2.1\n   686\t   should receive; the loader interface is resolved explicitly, not by\n   687\t   layout.\n   688\t3. **The nested grammar.** Versions 0, 1 and 2 remain compatibility\n   689\t   inputs. One canonical nested projection of version 3 is kept for\n   690\t   single-message channels and must stay exactly mutually inverse with\n   691\t   the rows. Rows are the carrier authority once specified. Retiring old\n   692\t   readers is an owner version-support decision.\n   693\t4. **Contract stamp.** The profile map and code contracts do not move; a\n   694\t   carrier change is a new body version with its own gate. Any change to\n   695\t   instruction or scheduler semantics would separately revise the\n   696\t   execution contract and is not proposed here.\n   697\t5. **Reified and parked.** Sharing immutable `:regs` rows is acceptable;\n   698\t   the two keep distinct outer kinds (§5.1) and distinct lowering. A\n   699\t   reified capture has no pending; a parked reference resolves in task\n   700\t   context.\n   701\t6. **Foreign-engine value profiles.** Required before any implementation\n   702\t   brief claims heterogeneous execution conformance, and not drafted\n   703\t   here: admitted values, numeric behaviour, key equality, metadata,\n   704\t   resource and profile requirements, refusal sets, and safepoint\n   705\t   reconstruction obligations. Slot losslessness alone does not\n   706\t   establish execution parity, and version 3, like version 2, does not\n   707\t   translate between kernels.\n   708\t\n   709\tAnswers 7–9 are the Architect's (round 2) and are adopted:\n   710\t\n   711\t7. **Other profile tables.** Deferral is acceptable for a semantic-only\n   712\t   Draft; version 3 declares semantic support only and the other\n   713\t   profiles fail closed at the gate (§2) until their complete tables and\n   714\t   validation rules are published, with their profiles and code\n   715\t   addresses unchanged.\n   716\t8. **Legacy readers.** Direct version-aware lowering of versions 0–2,\n   717\t   preserving address and custody binding; normalization is a separate,\n   718\t   explicit, partial tool (§9) and a normalized checkpoint has a\n   719\t   different address that never silently replaces an admitted legacy\n   720\t   one.\n   721\t9. **Skeleton placeholder.** Safe only if the full value-union tag is\n   722\t   retained in the skeleton, which §6 now requires (`[:cell <class>]`,\n   723\t   never a bare class); and a placeholder does not canonicalize alias\n   724\t   topology, which is why §6 is now defined as the output of a\n   725\t   renaming-invariant refinement-and-search procedure.\n   726\t\n   727\t10. **Receiver work bound** (Architect, round 3; adopted as a\n   728\t    requirement, with the limits left to implementation and\n   729\t    composition). Before an implementation brief, the receiver must have\n   730\t    a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cells, traversal depth, refinement\n   731\t    work and search nodes for §6, with elapsed time as an optional\n   732\t    operational limit on top. Exceeding a local budget produces a\n   733\t    structured resource or capability refusal before any attachment, in\n   734\t    the qualified-unsupported family `datom.world.md` already provides;\n   735\t    it never labels a valid state `:noncanonical-cells`, never falls\n   736\t    back to encounter order, and never accepts an unfinished check. The\n   737\t    exact limits and their refusal mapping are implementation and\n   738\t    composition decisions and must not alter canonical bytes.\n   739\t\n   740\t**Proposals, not consequences.** The following are design choices this\n   741\tdocument makes; they follow neither uniquely from the owner's three\n   742\trulings nor from the six invariants, and an implementation brief must\n   743\tname them as choices being accepted: a new body version (3) rather than\n   744\tanother carrier identification; the canonical representative of §6 as\n   745\tdefined by its procedure; the wire unit and pack framing of §3; the\n   746\texplicit fork-policy spelling of §5.5; semantic-only initial scope; the\n   747\tbyte-string and metadata restrictions of §4; and positional rows over\n   748\tEAV (§1).\n   749\t\n   750\tStill open after this revision: none beyond the other-profile tables\n   751\t(§10.7) and the work-bound limits (§10.10).\n   752\t\n   753\t## 11. What this does not change\n   754\t\n   755\tCode identity, the contract stamp and profile registry, safepoints,\n   756\tpending-wait semantics and the r5 required keys, install-child rules,\n   757\tthe dependency closure and satisfaction check, the isolated store, the\n   758\tprivate resource table, module-store instancing, scheduler state, the\n   759\tcustody header and admission rules, fencing and operation ids, the\n   760\toutcome families, and the existing refusal kinds (two lift kinds and two\n   761\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added, §8). The carrier is a new body\n   762\tversion for the same facts. The\n   763\tArchitect's round-1 statement is the acceptance bar: tuple transport\n   764\tviolates none of the six invariants once polymorphic references and\n   765\tcontext edges are specified and validated, and §4, §3 and §8 are where\n   766\tthey are.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git rev-parse HEAD; sed -n '430,498p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; rg -n 'defn.*request|request-op|request-args|envelope' src/cljc/dao/stream/apply* | head -30; sed -n '1,130p' src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git rev-parse HEAD; sed -n '430,498p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; rg -n 'defn.*request|request-op|request-args|envelope' src/cljc/dao/stream/apply* | head -30; sed -n '1,130p' src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"7e24fa3a7de016df2b4dcd00a9f74e30b1cf6cf8\nelsewhere in the task (the review's counterexample: `'a` bound to a set\nholding cells X and Y with equal bodies, `'b` bound to X; any rule that\nlooks only at the set and the bodies cannot tell X from Y, but `'b`\ncan). The numbering is therefore defined over the **whole alias\ntopology** of the task, and the definition is the reference against\nwhich any algorithm is checked.\n\n**What is defined, and what is not claimed.** The canonical numbering\nis defined as **the output of the procedure below**, which is a function\nof the state alone: every step depends only on structure, never on a\ncell's number or on any hash that includes one, so renaming the cells of\nthe input does not change the output. It is *not* claimed to be the\nminimum of the root bytes over all `n!` bijections; that is a different\ndefinition, and a refinement-and-search procedure is not shown to reach\nit (signature order does not order Merkle bytes). Nor is it claimed that\ncells left together by refinement are interchangeable: they need not be\n(a guest set encoding a disjoint triangle and square as ordered pairs of\nequal-body cells leaves all seven cells in one class, and no\nautomorphism exchanges a triangle vertex with a square vertex). The\nprocedure therefore explores every unresolved alternative, with no\npruning, and the representative it returns is canonical because the\nprocedure is deterministic and renaming-invariant, not because of any\nsymmetry argument.\n\n**The procedure.** Let the cells be `n` in number.\n\n1. **Class encoding.** A *class encoding* of a value is its canonical\n   bytes with every cell reference spelled `[:cell <class>]`, the full\n   value-union tag retained (§10.9), where `<class>` is the cell's\n   current class label; unordered containers inside it are ordered by\n   the class encodings of their elements; context lookups (parked ids,\n   store-of) are not expanded, they appear as the plain `P` or `M`. No\n   Merkle id enters a class encoding: rows are expanded structurally\n   for this purpose, and the expansion is bounded by the row DAG.\n2. **Refinement.** Initial class of a cell: the canonical bytes of its\n   body (stream marker, position). Repeat: for each cell, its new\n   signature is the pair *(old class, the sorted multiset of the\n   positions at which it is referenced)*, a position being the path from\n   the root in a fixed traversal (waits in order; within a wait\n   registers then pending; env bindings by symbol, stack bottom-up, k\n   bottom-to-top; parked table by `P`; store by key; module stores by\n   `M`; result; installs by module name with each child as its own task)\n   in which an unordered container's element is named by its class\n   encoding. New class labels are the signatures' canonical bytes,\n   ordered bytewise. Stop when the partition stops changing. Keeping\n   the old class in the signature makes refinement monotone, so the\n   partition stabilizes in at most `n − k` splitting rounds from `k`\n   initial classes plus one stability check; this bounds partition\n   rounds only, not signature-processing or search work.\n3. **Search.** If every class is a singleton, assign `1 … n` in class\n   order and stop. Otherwise take the first non-singleton class in\n   class order and, **for each of its members in turn**, give that\n   member a fresh singleton class ordered before all others, refine to\n   stability, and recurse. Every branch is explored. Each complete\n   leaf yields a numbering; build the §5 rows under it and take the\n   root's Merkle bytes as the leaf's key. The canonical numbering is the\n   leaf with the least key. Two leaves with equal keys have equal\n   complete commitments and are immaterial under the digest assumption.\n\n**Validation.** The receiver recomputes the procedure from the decoded\nrows and refuses with `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:noncanonical-cells` if\nthe carried numbering differs; contiguity alone is not canonicality.\nThis is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift kind. A receiver\nwhose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the procedure completes\nrefuses with a structured resource refusal before any attachment and\nnever labels the state `:noncanonical-cells`, never falls back to\nencounter order, and never accepts an unfinished check.\n\nUnder this rule two lifts of one state are byte-identical. Two lifts of\n2:  \"The request/response envelope for DaoStream v2.\n26:   policy belongs to whoever uses the envelope, not to this namespace.\"\n31:(defn request\n32:  \"Construct a request envelope.  Use `request?` to validate data received from\n38:(defn request?\n39:  \"True when value is a valid request envelope.  Unknown keys are intentionally\n40:   ignored so the envelope remains open.\"\n48:(defn request-id\n53:(defn request-op\n58:(defn request-args\n90:  \"True when value is a valid response envelope.  A response contains exactly\n141:(defn endpoint-request\n151:(defn put-request!\n152:  \"Append one validated request to an explicit writer handle.  Invalid envelope\n170:(defn next-request\n187:(defn dispatch-request\n197:                      \"Malformed request envelope\"))\n201:          op (request-op request-value)\n207:          (success-response id (apply handler (request-args request-value)))\n(ns dao.stream.apply\n  \"The request/response envelope for DaoStream v2.\n\n   This namespace owns only application data and one explicit server step.  It\n   neither creates streams nor retains handles, cursors, handlers, or requests\n   outside values supplied by its caller.  A driver owns the returned state and\n   decides when to call `serve-once!` again.\"\n  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Envelope\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def id-key :dao.stream.apply/id)\n(def op-key :dao.stream.apply/op)\n(def args-key :dao.stream.apply/args)\n(def ok-key :dao.stream.apply/ok)\n(def error-key :dao.stream.apply/error)\n(def code-key :dao.stream.apply/code)\n(def message-key :dao.stream.apply/message)\n\n\n(defn correlation-id?\n  \"True for a usable correlation id: any non-nil opaque value.  Its allocation\n   policy belongs to whoever uses the envelope, not to this namespace.\"\n  [id]\n  (some? id))\n\n\n(defn request\n  \"Construct a request envelope.  Use `request?` to validate data received from\n   a stream; constructors deliberately add no interpretation to arguments.\"\n  [id op args]\n  {id-key id op-key op args-key args})\n\n\n(defn request?\n  \"True when value is a valid request envelope.  Unknown keys are intentionally\n   ignored so the envelope remains open.\"\n  [value]\n  (and (map? value)\n       (correlation-id? (get value id-key))\n       (keyword? (get value op-key))\n       (vector? (get value args-key))))\n\n\n(defn request-id\n  [value]\n  (get value id-key))\n\n\n(defn request-op\n  [value]\n  (get value op-key))\n\n\n(defn request-args\n  [value]\n  (get value args-key))\n\n\n(defn error\n  \"Construct portable error data.  Codes are qualified keywords and messages\n   are ordinary strings, never host exception values.\"\n  [code message]\n  {code-key code message-key message})\n\n\n(defn error?\n  [value]\n  (and (map? value)\n       (stream/qualified-keyword?* (get value code-key))\n       (string? (get value message-key))))\n\n\n(defn success-response\n  \"Construct a successful response preserving the request id.\"\n  [id value]\n  {id-key id ok-key value})\n\n\n(defn error-response\n  \"Construct an error response preserving the request id.\"\n  [id code message]\n  {id-key id error-key (error code message)})\n\n\n(defn response?\n  \"True when value is a valid response envelope.  A response contains exactly\n   one of `:dao.stream.apply/ok` or `/error`; nil is a valid success value.\"\n  [value]\n  (and (map? value)\n       (correlation-id? (get value id-key))\n       (not= (contains? value ok-key) (contains? value error-key))\n       (or (contains? value ok-key)\n           (error? (get value error-key)))))\n\n\n(defn response-id\n  [value]\n  (get value id-key))\n\n\n(defn response-ok\n  [value]\n  (get value ok-key))\n\n\n(defn response-error\n  [value]\n  (get value error-key))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Endpoint descriptors and explicit stream helpers\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def request-key :dao.stream.apply/request)\n(def response-key :dao.stream.apply/response)\n\n\n(defn endpoint\n  \"Construct a portable endpoint pair from request and response descriptors.\n   Resolving descriptors into handles is host composition and stays outside this\n   namespace.\"\n  [request-descriptor response-descriptor]\n  {request-key request-descriptor response-key response-descriptor})\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'defn code-address|algorithm|sha256|segment-key' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc | head -20; sed -n '100,119p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '315,339p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '65,83p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc; rg -n 'default-hash-algorithm' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | head; git diff HEAD~1 HEAD -- docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md | sed -n '180,330p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'defn code-address|algorithm|sha256|segment-key' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc | head -20; sed -n '100,119p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '315,339p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; sed -n '65,83p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc; rg -n 'default-hash-algorithm' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | head; git diff HEAD~1 HEAD -- docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md | sed -n '180,330p'\"","aggregated_output":"14:     of that vector is `(dao.jing/segment-key v)`; the execution contract\n247:(defn code-address\n251:  (jing/segment-key v))\nany traversal (`:yin.k/undecodable`), so every walk is over a map from\nid to one row. The gate's walk over install children follows only\n`:install` child crefs and keeps an **active recursion-path set**: a\ncref that leaves the rows, or a child that is already on the active\npath, stops the gate with `:yin.k/undecodable`. A child reached again\n*off* the active path is not an error: two install entries whose\nchildren have identical content (two identical halted children, say)\nshare one child row by Merkle construction, the structural graph is a\nDAG, and each install entry still restores as its own native task\ninstance with its own task-local cells and its own contextual custody\nvalidation. Completed content validation is cached per row; instance\nrestoration never is. When a pack is addressed (§10.2) this map is what\nis hashed, and a `:yin.k/root` that does not name a `:task` row in\n`:rows` is `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n\n**Content rows** have `id = (dao.jing/segment-key body)` under the one\ndigest this carrier pins, SHA-256, the same digest the semantic profile's\ncode addresses `:segment/sha256-…` use; an id carrying any other\nmultihash is `:yin.k/undecodable`. The carrier fixes the digest rather\nthan dispatching on each supplied address, because a canonical address\n| `:ffi` | `cell-C  call-id-D  op` (op may be nil) |\n| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D  envelope-cref  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  response-cell-C  op-id-D` |\n| `:link-request` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C  envelope-D  op-id-D` |\n| `:link-response` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C` |\n| `:install` | `name-S` |\n\nThe retained FFI request is a closed content row:\n\n| Kind | Slots |\n|---|---|\n| `:ffi-envelope` | `call-id-D  op  args-cref` where `args-cref` targets a `:vec` row (or the slot holds `[:lit []]` when there are no arguments) |\n\nso that the arguments are guest values encoded recursively, closures\nstay portable, and resource references enter the census. Lower\nreconstructs the retained request verbatim from these fields and never\nrecomputes the arguments. Correlation must agree, not merely be present:\na cell's `:cell-body` stream is the pending's response stream; an\nenvelope's link id or call id equals the pending's; a `:link-request`\nenvelope names the pending's module. `op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\npresent exactly when the write was first attempted through a fenced\nwriter, carried verbatim, never minted or renumbered by lift or lower,\nand subject to every rule of §7.4.3 r5 (below the root's\n`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, unique across the task and its children, absent\nwhen the body has no origin). `request-envelope` and `envelope` are the\nretained requests, retried verbatim. `call-id` and `link-id` are plain.\n              :yin.code/contract vm/register-contract\n              :yin.k/version 1}\n   :walker {:yin.k/engine :walker\n            :yin.code/contract vm/ast-contract\n            :yin.k/version 1}})\n\n\n(def supported-profiles\n  \"Every profile map a version-2 reader of this composition accepts.\"\n  (set (vals profiles)))\n\n\n(defn profile-engine\n  \"The engine of `profile` when it is exactly one registry entry, the\n   inner version compared on the canonical integer kind so a float\n   carrier never equals its integral value; nil otherwise.\"\n  [profile]\n  (when (and (map? profile) (= 3 (count profile)))\n    (let [engine (:yin.k/engine profile)\n88:(def default-hash-algorithm\n173:   default-hash-algorithm, i.e. :blake3). Returns lowercase hexadecimal string.\n176:   (content-hash v {:algorithm default-hash-algorithm}))\n178:   (let [algo (clojure.core/get opts :algorithm default-hash-algorithm)]\n185:   The 1-arg form defaults to default-hash-algorithm (:blake3).\n188:   (segment-key v {:algorithm default-hash-algorithm}))\n190:   (let [algo (clojure.core/get opts :algorithm default-hash-algorithm)\n375:   Supports [handle payload] (defaulting to default-hash-algorithm, :blake3)\n396:   (materialize! handle payload {:algorithm default-hash-algorithm}))\n406:     (let [algo (clojure.core/get opts :algorithm default-hash-algorithm)\n+a cell's `:cell-body` stream is the pending's response stream; an\n+envelope's link id or call id equals the pending's; a `:link-request`\n+envelope names the pending's module. `op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\n present exactly when the write was first attempted through a fenced\n writer, carried verbatim, never minted or renumbered by lift or lower,\n and subject to every rule of §7.4.3 r5 (below the root's\n@@ -314,7 +346,7 @@ is `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n \n | Kind | Slots |\n |---|---|\n-| `:store` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …` sorted by the key's cell-free skeleton; the isolated slice of UCF §7.6.2 |\n+| `:store` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …` sorted by key; the isolated slice of UCF §7.6.2 |\n | `:module-store` | `manifest-M  store-cref` |\n | `:parked-table` | `P₁ regs-cref₁ P₂ regs-cref₂ …` sorted by P |\n | `:install` | `name-S  phase  parent-D  response-D  child-cref` |\n@@ -402,51 +434,66 @@ can). The numbering is therefore defined over the **whole alias\n topology** of the task, and the definition is the reference against\n which any algorithm is checked.\n \n-**Definition.** Let the task's cells be `n` in number. For each bijection\n-`σ` from the cells to `{1 … n}`, build the complete row set of §5 with\n-cell `c` spelled `:yin.k/c-σ(c)` (ordered containers in order; sets,\n-maps, stores, bindings and tables sorted by the canonical bytes of their\n-keys *as spelled under σ*), and take the canonical bytes of the root\n-body. The canonical numbering is the `σ` whose root bytes are least\n-under bytewise order. Two `σ` with equal least bytes are automorphisms\n-of the task (they produce identical row sets by construction) and the\n-choice between them is immaterial by definition, not by assumption.\n-\n-**Algorithm.** The lift computes the same `σ` without enumerating all\n-`n!` bijections:\n-\n-1. **Refinement.** Give every cell the signature of its body (stream,\n-   position). Repeat: encode the task with each cell spelled by its\n-   current signature class, in a fixed traversal (waits in order; within\n-   a wait registers then pending; env bindings by symbol, stack\n-   bottom-up, k bottom-to-top; parked table by `P`; store by key;\n-   module stores by `M`; result; installs by name with each child as\n-   its own task), and give each cell the new signature *(old class, the\n-   multiset of positions at which it is referenced, where a position is\n-   the path from the root with unordered containers' elements named by\n-   their own current encoding)*. Stop when the partition into classes\n-   stops changing. This is colour refinement over the reference graph\n-   and terminates in at most `n` rounds.\n-2. **Individualization.** If every class is a singleton, order classes\n-   by their signature's canonical bytes and assign `1 … n`. Otherwise\n-   pick the smallest class under that order, try each of its members as\n-   the next individualized cell, refine again, and recurse; among the\n-   resulting complete numberings take the one with the least root\n-   bytes. Residual classes after full refinement are orbits of the\n-   task's automorphism group, so every choice within a class yields the\n-   same root bytes and the search is bounded by the symmetry of the\n-   state, which for a parked task is small.\n-\n-Skeletons used inside the refinement keep the full value-union tags:\n-a cell position is spelled `[:cell <class>]`, never a bare class, so it\n-cannot collide with `[:lit [:cell …]]` (§10.9). Context lookups (parked\n-ids, store-of) are not expanded during refinement; they are followed\n-once as edges to their target rows, which are already in the traversal.\n-\n-**Validation.** The receiver recomputes `σ` from the decoded rows and\n-refuses with `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:noncanonical-cells` if the\n-carried numbering differs; contiguity alone is not canonicality. This\n-is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift kind.\n+**What is defined, and what is not claimed.** The canonical numbering\n+is defined as **the output of the procedure below**, which is a function\n+of the state alone: every step depends only on structure, never on a\n+cell's number or on any hash that includes one, so renaming the cells of\n+the input does not change the output. It is *not* claimed to be the\n+minimum of the root bytes over all `n!` bijections; that is a different\n+definition, and a refinement-and-search procedure is not shown to reach\n+it (signature order does not order Merkle bytes). Nor is it claimed that\n+cells left together by refinement are interchangeable: they need not be\n+(a guest set encoding a disjoint triangle and square as ordered pairs of\n+equal-body cells leaves all seven cells in one class, and no\n+automorphism exchanges a triangle vertex with a square vertex). The\n+procedure therefore explores every unresolved alternative, with no\n+pruning, and the representative it returns is canonical because the\n+procedure is deterministic and renaming-invariant, not because of any\n+symmetry argument.\n+\n+**The procedure.** Let the cells be `n` in number.\n+\n+1. **Class encoding.** A *class encoding* of a value is its canonical\n+   bytes with every cell reference spelled `[:cell <class>]`, the full\n+   value-union tag retained (§10.9), where `<class>` is the cell's\n+   current class label; unordered containers inside it are ordered by\n+   the class encodings of their elements; context lookups (parked ids,\n+   store-of) are not expanded, they appear as the plain `P` or `M`. No\n+   Merkle id enters a class encoding: rows are expanded structurally\n+   for this purpose, and the expansion is bounded by the row DAG.\n+2. **Refinement.** Initial class of a cell: the canonical bytes of its\n+   body (stream marker, position). Repeat: for each cell, its new\n+   signature is the pair *(old class, the sorted multiset of the\n+   positions at which it is referenced)*, a position being the path from\n+   the root in a fixed traversal (waits in order; within a wait\n+   registers then pending; env bindings by symbol, stack bottom-up, k\n+   bottom-to-top; parked table by `P`; store by key; module stores by\n+   `M`; result; installs by module name with each child as its own task)\n+   in which an unordered container's element is named by its class\n+   encoding. New class labels are the signatures' canonical bytes,\n+   ordered bytewise. Stop when the partition stops changing. Keeping\n+   the old class in the signature makes refinement monotone, so the\n+   partition stabilizes in at most `n − k` splitting rounds from `k`\n+   initial classes plus one stability check; this bounds partition\n+   rounds only, not signature-processing or search work.\n+3. **Search.** If every class is a singleton, assign `1 … n` in class\n+   order and stop. Otherwise take the first non-singleton class in\n+   class order and, **for each of its members in turn**, give that\n+   member a fresh singleton class ordered before all others, refine to\n+   stability, and recurse. Every branch is explored. Each complete\n+   leaf yields a numbering; build the §5 rows under it and take the\n+   root's Merkle bytes as the leaf's key. The canonical numbering is the\n+   leaf with the least key. Two leaves with equal keys have equal\n+   complete commitments and are immaterial under the digest assumption.\n+\n+**Validation.** The receiver recomputes the procedure from the decoded\n+rows and refuses with `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:noncanonical-cells` if\n+the carried numbering differs; contiguity alone is not canonicality.\n+This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift kind. A receiver\n+whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the procedure completes\n+refuses with a structured resource refusal before any attachment and\n+never labels the state `:noncanonical-cells`, never falls back to\n+encounter order, and never accepts an unfinished check.\n \n Under this rule two lifts of one state are byte-identical. Two lifts of\n different states sharing a closure over the same cell may number it\n@@ -494,35 +541,42 @@ one attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n    and all profiles equal to the root's. Malformed task structure is\n    `:yin.k/undecodable`; an unsupported version or profile is\n    `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; the two are never conflated.\n-2. **Structure.** Every row's kind is tabled with the right arity; every\n-   structural slot holds its tabled kind; every `[:row B]` targets a row\n-   of a kind admissible in that position; every `V` is in §4's union,\n-   every literal in the portable domain, every `D` in the canonical\n-   bytes domain; exactly one row has role `:root`; kind rules of §5.5\n-   hold; the custody slot matches the role, kind and policy table.\n-3. **Canonical form.** Every sorted slot is in its tabled order; every\n-   keyed row has unique keys under canonical bytes and under portable\n-   decoded equality; optional absence has its one spelling (nil in the\n-   tabled slot); a reference-free collection is spelled `[:lit d]`, never\n-   a row; empty collections are literals; metadata obeys §4. A breach is\n-   `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row and slot. Without this step two\n-   different valid encodings could lower to one state, or a `:bindings`\n-   row could carry one symbol twice and lose a binding on lower.\n-4. **Hashes.** Every content row's id equals the address of its body\n-   under the digest the id's multihash names; every code payload hashes\n-   to its key `A` and is well formed under the stamp; the requested body\n-   address, if one was given, equals the root id. Mismatch is\n-   `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the row or code key.\n-5. **Graph.** The structural edge family is acyclic; every row is\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-09 17:40:58 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-10 00:40:58 Asia/Bangkok\n\nReviewed `7e24fa3a7de016df2b4dcd00a9f74e30b1cf6cf8`, the requested diff and sections, plus the relevant envelope and addressing code. No files edited.\n\nThe revised numbering approach can define a canonical representation without claiming an unrestricted minimum. Its exact procedure still needs specification, and the FFI envelope and gate contain concrete remaining conflicts.\n\n1. **HIGH — schema conflict | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:325 | Ordinary FFI arguments have no valid representation.** A retained request with arguments `[1 2]` must use a `:vec` row here, but §4 and validation step 5 forbid reference-free collection rows. Only empty arguments have a literal alternative. | Make the argument slot a `V` restricted to vectors: `[:lit [1 2]]` for reference-free arguments and `[:row B]` targeting `:vec` otherwise. This also removes the special empty-vector exception.\n\n2. **HIGH — pending-state completeness | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:321 | Three fields cannot reconstruct every retained request verbatim.** `dao.stream.apply/request?` explicitly permits unknown envelope keys (`src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:38`). The existing handoff encoder encodes the complete retained envelope, and lower decodes it. A closed call-id/op/args row drops additional keys and envelope metadata. | Preserve the complete recursively encoded envelope, or add an explicit extension/metadata representation to the closed row. Do not silently narrow an open request protocol while claiming unchanged pending semantics.\n\n3. **HIGH — contradictory validation rule | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:536 | Step 1 still rejects every revisit.** §3 correctly permits off-path shared children, but the binding validation sequence still says “with a visited set” and “a revisit … stops the gate.” Thus the round-3 shared-child correction is only partially applied. | Replace this wording with the active-path cycle rule from §3. Cache completed content validation separately; retain contextual validation and restoration per install occurrence.\n\n4. **MEDIUM — canonicalization specification gap | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:455 | Not every input to the procedure is fixed.** Class encoding lacks a concrete structural grammar. Maps need an exact entry encoding and ordering rule; class substitution can make distinct keys/elements equal, so multiplicities must survive without constructing collapsing host maps/sets. Paths need explicit slot and key/value discriminants. “Fresh singleton class ordered before all others” does not specify its encoding or how that ordering survives bytewise signature ordering. “Root’s Merkle bytes” also leaves root-body bytes versus digest bytes ambiguous. | Publish exact encodings for classes, signatures, paths, individualization markers, and leaf keys. Use synthetic ordered structures that preserve repeated class encodings. Specify task-boundary handling and zero-cell behavior. With those choices fixed invariantly, exhaustive branching and minimum leaf selection establish canonical output; the present prose is not yet a reproducible byte contract.\n\n5. **MEDIUM — canonicality-check ambiguity | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:489 | Equal commitments do not select a unique source-cell labeling.** Automorphic leaves may assign different numbers to individual input cells while producing exactly the same emitted rows. That makes equal leaf keys immaterial for the transported representation, but not for a check that literally compares “the carried numbering” with one arbitrarily selected labeling. | Define the output as the canonical rooted encoding. Validate by rebuilding and comparing canonical rows/root commitment, rather than comparing one source-cell-to-number bijection. Alternatively, accept every labeling yielding the selected commitment. No encounter-order tie-break should decide validity.\n\n6. **MEDIUM — addressing/provenance | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:116 | The SHA-256 rationale and API expression disagree with current code.** `ucf/code-address` calls one-argument `jing/segment-key`, whose current default is BLAKE3. Therefore SHA-256 is not necessarily “the same digest” as semantic code, and the displayed one-argument row-address expression does not implement the new pin. | Spell row hashing as `segment-key body {:algorithm :sha256}`. Keep existing code addresses under their governing address function. Remove the claimed digest equivalence and add the carrier digest selection to §10’s proposed choices.\n\nProperties that passed:\n\n- The procedure no longer claims unrestricted minimization or equates refinement classes with automorphism orbits.\n- Monotone refinement supports the stated `n−k` splitting-round bound plus a stability check. This correctly bounds partitions, not processing or search work.\n- Exhaustive branching avoids reliance on unresolved cells being interchangeable.\n- Equal leaf commitments make ties immaterial **for the emitted state**, under the digest assumption and complete census commitment.\n- Hash-free structural expansion and full value-union tags remove dependence on incoming cell numbers and cell-dependent row hashes, provided finding 4 fixes their exact encoding.\n- §3 and restoration step 12 correctly separate shared immutable child content from separate native task instances.\n- Emitted-row ordering now has one rule; skeletons are confined to refinement.\n- Metadata restrictions are explicit and correctly identified as proposed carrier limitations.\n- Reference completeness and acyclicity now precede recursive canonical-form checks. Set-element uniqueness and receiver work bounds are included.\n- The census, disjoint value union, task-local resources, isolated restoration, custody checks, and transport/native-execution boundary continue to satisfy the intended six-invariant architecture.\n\nThe validation phase order is now appropriate. I cannot certify step 12 for **every accepted input** until the executable schema and canonicality checks are made consistent, particularly findings 1, 4, and 5.\n\n§10’s proposal list is otherwise adequate. Add the digest pin; any decision to reject or discard previously permitted FFI envelope extensions would also be a new protocol decision, not an inherited carrier requirement. Preserving those fields needs no such semantic change.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED — fix findings 1–3 before publication; specify 4–5 before claiming canonicality; reconcile 6 and the proposal list. Merge publishes a Draft."}}
